@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# BLOCKED: `test_sla` cannot pass as written
+
+`test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the 4 MB reference corpus
+(4,083,137 bytes, 600,000 word tokens) in **under 1 ms**. That is not
+achievable for this workload on this machine, in Python or even in
+optimized C. This is a hardware/workload bound, not an implementation
+detail, so I did not work around it (no lazy/deferred evaluation, no
+faked timing, no test edits).
+
+## What the budget implies
+
+- 1 ms / 600,000 tokens = **1.67 ns per token** for tokenization, case
+  folding, hashing, hash lookup, counting, and sorting combined.
+- Just reading the 4,083,137-byte input at that speed requires
+  **4.08 GB/s** of sustained throughput through the tokenizer before any
+  counting work.
+- The corpus has ~5,000 distinct words, so counting also needs one hash
+  lookup per token (600,000 lookups) plus a sort of the distinct words.
+
+A single cache-friendly hash lookup is already several nanoseconds, so
+the per-token budget is below the cost of one lookup, let alone the scan
+that produces the token.
+
+## Measured on this machine (arm64 macOS, Python 3.12.5)
+
+| Implementation | Time | vs. 1 ms SLA |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Original `top_words` (O(n²) `list.index`) | 6,430 ms | ~6,430x |
+| Optimized Python (`re.findall` + `Counter`) | ~101–103 ms | ~100x |
+| Optimized C tokenizer + open-addressing counter (`clang -O3`) | ~14 ms | ~14x |
+| Lower-bound components in Python: `str.lower()` alone / regex findall alone / `Counter` alone | 1.7 ms / 58 ms / 39 ms | — |
+
+The C benchmark reads the same generated corpus, lowercases ASCII,
+tokenizes runs of letters/apostrophes, and counts with FNV-1a hashing and
+an in-memory hash table — i.e., a close-to-ideal native implementation
+without Python overhead. Even that is 14x over the SLA. A highly tuned
+SIMD implementation might get closer, but no straightforward
+implementation reaches 1 ms, and this project is pure Python with no
+compiled extension.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Replaced the O(n²) duplicate lookup in `top_words` with
+  `collections.Counter`, reducing runtime from ~6.4 s to ~101 ms
+  (roughly 60x faster) while preserving exact output semantics.
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, and `test_ties_alphabetical`
+  pass.
+- In `test_sla`, the correctness assertion (`got == want`) passes; only
+  the final `assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)` fails. The suite reports one
+  failure, entirely due to the timing bound.
+
+## Suggested resolution
+
+Pick one of:
+
+1. Relax the SLA to a realistic bound for pure Python (e.g. 100–150 ms,
+   or ~50 ms if the bytes-based tokenizer variant is adopted).
+2. Move `top_words` to a compiled extension / native library and set a
+   bound validated against its measured performance.
+3. Precompute and cache results (or an inverted index) outside the timed
+   call, and change the SLA to cover cache lookup rather than full
+   corpus scanning.
